'''Shirakawa Hakuōke''' (白川伯王家), or simply '''Shirakawa-ke''' (白川家), was a {{ill|Kuge|ja|公家}} (court noble) and later a {{ill|Kazoku|ja|華族}} (peerage) family in Japan. It originated from {{ill|Emperor Kazan|ja|花山天皇}}’s grandson, {{ill|Prince Nobunaga|ja|延信王}}, who was appointed {{ill|Jingi Haku|ja|神祇伯}} (Chief Priest of the Jingi-kan). His descendants later inherited the position of Jingi Haku. Although technically a branch of the {{ill|Minamoto clan|ja|源氏}} (Kazan Genji), the family maintained their status as {{ill|Ōji|ja|王氏}} (royal clan) during the tenure of each Jingi Haku, reflecting their unique position. 

The family’s rank among kuge was classified as {{ill|Half-house (Kuge)|ja|半家}}, while under the kazoku system, they were a {{ill|Viscount|ja|子爵}} house. They served as the head family of {{ill|Haku-ke Shintō|ja|伯家神道}} (Shirakawa-ryū Shintō), overseeing {{ill|Imperial Court rituals|ja|宮中祭祀}}. The Jingi Haku’s return to the ōji title upon appointment was a distinctive aspect of their status.

== History ==
### Establishment of the Shirakawa Hakuōke
The Shirakawa family’s unique feature was the inheritance of the Jingi Haku title, along with the use of the title {{ill|Ō|ja|王}}. The title of ō was determined by the degree of kinship with the emperor, typically not associated with any official position. The Shirakawa family’s ability to inherit the title despite being non-imperial was unprecedented. Below is an explanation of the family’s establishment with this context in mind.

Prince Nobunaga, born to {{ill|Prince Kiyohito|ja|清仁親王}}, was granted the Minamoto surname in {{ill|Manju|ja|万寿}} 2 (1025) and descended from the imperial family. In {{ill|Kantoku|ja|寛徳}} 3 (1046), he was appointed Jingi Haku. 

His son, {{ill|Prince Kōshi|ja|康資王}}, continued the Jingi Haku lineage and was recognized as the grandson of Emperor Kazan due to his father’s adoption into the imperial line. However, the exclusive inheritance of the Jingi Haku title by the family did not solidify until later generations.

The system of appointing Jingi Haku underwent changes over the next few generations, with notable figures such as {{ill|Prince Kōshi|ja|康資王}}, {{ill|Minamoto no Akinaka|ja|源顕仲}}, and members of the {{ill|Ōnakatomi clan|ja|大中臣氏}} also serving as Jingi Haku.

The consolidation of the title within the family began with {{ill|Minamoto no Akiyasu|ja|源顕康}}, who was adopted by the {{ill|Murakami Genji|ja|村上源氏}}. His son, {{ill|Prince Akihiro|ja|顕広王}}, played a pivotal role, being regarded as a third-generation prince (三世王). Akihiro’s appointment as Jingi Haku and his return to the ōji title set a precedent for subsequent generations.

